}i/{\textgreater}{\textless}sub{\textgreater}peak{\textless}sub/{\textgreater} = (1.0 ± 0.2)×10{\textless}sup{\textgreater}−3{\textless}sup/{\textgreater} and FWHM = 31.2 ± 8.3 km s{\textless}sup{\textgreater}−1{\textless}sup/{\textgreater} was found at {\textless}i{\textgreater}z{\textless}i/{\textgreater} = 1.124. This corresponds to a velocity offset of −9965 km s{\textless}sup{\textgreater}−1{\textless}sup/{\textgreater} with respect to the systemic redshift of 3C 190.{\textless}i{\textgreater}Conclusions.{\textless}i/{\textgreater} We consider three interpretations of the origin of the RRL emission: an intervening dwarf-like galaxy, an active galactic nucleus (AGN) driven outflow, and the inter-galactic medium. We argue that the recombination lines most likely originate in a dwarf-like galaxy ({\textless}i{\textgreater}M{\textless}i/{\textgreater} ∼ 10{\textless}sup{\textgreater}9{\textless}sup/{\textgreater} {\textless}i{\textgreater}M{\textless}i/{\textgreater}{\textless}sub{\textgreater}⊙{\textless}sub/{\textgreater}) along the line of sight, although we cannot rule out an AGN-driven outflow. We do find the RRLs to be inconsistent with an inter-galactic medium origin. With this detection, we have opened up a new way to study the physical properties of cool, diffuse gas out to cosmological distances.},
